                "You offer it up. She turns it over twice, works it into a gap in the cairn at about knee height and thumbs a stone down over it.\n\n'That's yours in there now,' she says. 'Everything in this heap was carried up by somebody who then went down that cave. Most of them I never saw come back. It isn't much of a grave and it isn't much of a shop, but it's what I keep.'\n\nFrom inside her coat she brings out a small clay flask with wax over the stopper and puts it in your hand without being asked. 'Resin and bitterroot. It will close a wound, the once. Don't save it for a better day. There isn't a better day.'"
